            "Welcome to Wild Minds, the podcast about the surprising lives of animals. Today we're looking at honeybees, and trust me, they're far more organised than most offices. A single hive can contain tens of thousands of bees, but only one of them is the queen. Her main job is to lay eggs, sometimes well over a thousand in a single day. Almost all the other bees are female workers, and what they do depends on their age. Young workers stay inside, cleaning cells and feeding the larvae. Older ones become foragers, flying out to collect nectar and pollen. Now here's my favourite part. When a forager finds a good patch of flowers, she returns to the hive and performs what scientists call the waggle dance. The angle of her dance shows the direction of the flowers compared to the sun, and the length of the dance tells the others how far away they are. It's essentially a map made of movement. After the break, we'll talk to a beekeeper about why bee numbers have been falling, and what gardeners can do to help.",
