        new("l-c1-1", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Podcast: Why Habits Stick",
            "Welcome back to The Curious Mind. Today we're exploring a question that has probably frustrated every single one of you at some point. Why is it so hard to build good habits, and so annoyingly easy to keep bad ones? Researchers who study behaviour describe a habit as a kind of loop. There's a cue, something in your environment that triggers the behaviour. There's the routine itself. And there's a reward, which is what persuades your brain that the whole thing is worth repeating. The crucial point is that once a habit is established, it runs largely on autopilot. That's wonderful when the habit is brushing your teeth, and rather less wonderful when it's reaching for your phone the moment you feel bored. Contrary to the popular claim that it takes exactly twenty one days to form a habit, the research suggests the timeframe varies enormously, from a few weeks to the better part of a year, depending on the person and the behaviour. Later in the episode, I'll be speaking to a psychologist who argues that willpower is overrated, and that the smartest strategy is to redesign your surroundings so that the good choice becomes the easy one. Stay with us.",
