            "Good afternoon. In today's lecture, I want to consider a pattern so familiar that we rarely question it. If you look at a map of the world's great historic cities, a remarkable number of them sit on the banks of a river. Why should that be? The obvious answer is drinking water, but that's only the beginning. Rivers deposit fertile soil when they flood, which allowed early farmers to produce a surplus of food. And a surplus, crucially, is what frees some people from farming altogether, so they can become craftsmen, traders and officials. In other words, city dwellers. Rivers were also the motorways of the ancient world. Moving heavy goods by boat was vastly cheaper and faster than hauling them overland, so places where routes met, or where a river could easily be crossed, tended to flourish as markets. Here in Central Asia, the oasis cities along the Zarafshan depended on careful irrigation for their very existence. That said, rivers were a mixed blessing. The same floods that enriched the soil could sweep away homes, and standing water brought disease. So next week, we'll look at how early engineers tried to control rivers, with varying degrees of success.",
