        new("l-c1-3", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Radio Essay: In Praise of Boredom",
            "When did you last do absolutely nothing? I don't mean scrolling through your phone while waiting for a bus. I mean genuinely sitting with no entertainment at all. For many of us, the honest answer is that we can't remember. We've become remarkably efficient at eliminating boredom, and I want to suggest tonight that this may come at a hidden cost. Boredom, after all, is uncomfortable for a reason. It's a signal that our current situation isn't satisfying, a nudge to go and find something more meaningful to do. When we instantly silence that signal with a screen, we never discover where it might have led. There's also intriguing evidence linking boredom to creativity. In one study, participants who first completed a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers out of a phone book, later came up with more imaginative ideas than those who hadn't. The theory is that a bored mind starts to wander, and a wandering mind is where unexpected connections are made. I'm not suggesting we should seek out misery. But perhaps, the next time you're standing in a queue, you might resist the urge to reach for your pocket, and simply see where your thoughts take you.",
            [
                new("What is the speaker's main message?", ["Phones should be banned in public places", "Boredom is usually a sign of illness", "Boredom can be useful, so we should not always avoid it", "Queues in modern cities are getting longer"], 2, "The speaker suggests that \"eliminating boredom\" \"may come at a hidden cost\"."),
                new("According to the speaker, what is boredom a signal of?", ["That our current situation is not satisfying", "That we need more sleep", "That we are hungry", "That we are working too hard"], 0, "The speaker says, \"It's a signal that our current situation isn't satisfying.\""),
                new("What task did participants in the study do first?", ["They read a long novel", "They played a video game", "They sat in silence for an hour", "They copied numbers out of a phone book"], 3, "The speaker describes \"a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers out of a phone book\"."),
                new("What does the speaker suggest doing in a queue?", ["Reading the news", "Resisting the phone and letting your thoughts wander", "Calling a friend", "Planning the rest of your day"], 1, "The speaker suggests you \"resist the urge to reach for your pocket, and simply see where your thoughts take you\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-4", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Radio Interview: The Chef Who Wastes Nothing",
            "Good evening, and welcome to Kitchen Table. My guest tonight runs a small restaurant with an unusual promise. Nothing that comes into her kitchen goes into the bin. Not the carrot tops, not the stale bread, not even the coffee grounds. Now, you might assume this is simply a clever marketing idea, but when I visited her last month, I was genuinely struck by how seriously she takes it. Vegetable peelings become stock. Yesterday's bread is turned into a crisp crumb for salads or a rather wonderful dessert. Fruit that's past its best ends up in jams and drinks, and whatever truly can't be eaten is composted for the farm that supplies her vegetables. She tells me the approach began out of necessity rather than principle. When she opened, money was so tight that she simply couldn't afford to throw anything away. Only later did she realise how much food restaurants typically waste, and how much flavour was hiding in the parts most cooks discard. Tonight she'll share some of her techniques, and she's also going to challenge us, the home cooks, to rethink what we consider rubbish. So if you've got a notebook handy, I'd suggest keeping it close.",
            [
                new("What is the presenter mainly doing?", ["Introducing a chef whose restaurant avoids wasting food", "Reviewing an expensive restaurant", "Explaining how to grow vegetables on a farm", "Warning listeners about unhealthy coffee"], 0, "The presenter says the guest's promise is that \"nothing that comes into her kitchen goes into the bin\"."),
                new("What happens to yesterday's bread?", ["It is thrown away", "It is given to the farm", "It becomes a crumb for salads or a dessert", "It is used to make stock"], 2, "The presenter says, \"Yesterday's bread is turned into a crisp crumb for salads or a rather wonderful dessert.\""),
                new("Why did the chef first start avoiding waste?", ["She wanted good publicity", "She could not afford to throw anything away", "A customer asked her to", "A cooking school taught her to"], 1, "The presenter says \"money was so tight that she simply couldn't afford to throw anything away\"."),
                new("What will the chef challenge listeners to do?", ["Open their own restaurants", "Stop eating bread", "Visit her farm", "Rethink what they consider rubbish"], 3, "The presenter says she will \"challenge us, the home cooks, to rethink what we consider rubbish\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-5", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Talk: Losing the Night Sky",
            "Thank you all for coming. I'd like to begin with a simple question. When did you last see the Milky Way? If you live in a city, the answer may well be never. Astronomers estimate that a large share of the world's population now lives under skies so brightened by artificial light that our own galaxy is effectively invisible. This is what we call light pollution, and unlike many environmental problems, it's remarkably easy to overlook, precisely because it's the absence of something. Yet its effects reach far beyond disappointed stargazers. Migrating birds, which navigate partly by the stars, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings. Newly hatched sea turtles, which instinctively head towards the brightest horizon, may crawl inland towards streetlights instead of towards the sea. And in humans, bright light late at night can disrupt the body clock and interfere with sleep. The encouraging part is that, unlike most forms of pollution, this one can be reversed almost instantly. Shielding streetlights so they point downwards, choosing warmer colours and simply switching off lights that nobody needs can make a dramatic difference. Several towns have already been recognised for protecting their dark skies, and visitors now travel specifically to see them.",
            [
                new("What is the talk mainly about?", ["How to become an astronomer", "Why cities need more streetlights", "The migration routes of sea turtles", "The effects of light pollution and how to reduce it"], 3, "The speaker introduces \"what we call light pollution\", describes its effects and then its solutions."),
